ion dollars or  20 million dollars to support a  particular candidate but according to  the FEC what there are you in court is  once that person gets together with  someone else then neither of them can  spend more than five thousand dollars on  the effort so this seems like a very odd  rule and it's more than infringes on  freedom of association and a lot of  people say well you know still that only  really affects fat cats you know who can  afford to get more than five thousand  dollars you know but but part of the the  issue in speech now is that many of the  members of speech now are small donors  who cannot give large sums and what  gives their voice power what allows them  to be heard in a political system is  being able to join together with  wealthier individuals who can contribute  a hundred thousand dollars or fifty  thousand dollars into the campaign  that's what makes them heard and that's  what the current law prevents  let me ask you this question imagine if  George Bush were to announced here in  the fading twilight of his presidency  that in order to prevent terrorists from  infiltrating American political parties  in the succeeding control or American  government we need to have we need to  introduce the Patriot to act and the  Patriot to act would require citizens to  report to the government their political  activities and the government would keep  that in a database which by the way they  would then make available to private  individuals like employers or maybe  groups that might want to protest  outside your home as to what your  political activities were what do you  think would be the reaction to that law  well I don't know I know some people i  think would support there was a great  idea I know on America's political left  would go berserk they would go  absolutely nuts well you know what we  have that law already and it's called  campaign finance it's called the federal  election campaign act which requires you  to report to the government 